    "boston-roxbury-grant-lawsuit-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The city says the U.S. Department of Transportation unlawfully withdrew a 2022 award for safety, transit and accessibility work on three major corridors.",
      "id": "boston-roxbury-grant-lawsuit-2026",
      "text": "boston sues over $20m grant for roxbury street upgrades the city says the u.s. department of transportation unlawfully withdrew a 2022 award for safety, transit and accessibility work on three major corridors. the city of boston has gone to federal court to recover a $20 million transportation grant that officials say was withdrawn after the project included a small electric-vehicle charging component. the lawsuit, filed sept. 28 in the u.s. district court for the district of massachusetts, asks the court to reverse the u.s. department of transportation's cancellation of the 2022 award for the roxbury resilient corridors project. the filing and case number, city of boston v. duffy, are listed in the public docket record (https://dockets.justia.com/docket/massachusetts/madce/1:2026cv14419/306825). the dispute centers on three roxbury streets: melnea cass boulevard, malcolm x boulevard and warren street. boston's project page says the grant was intended to support street repairs, safer crossings, transit improvements, accessibility work, traffic-flow changes, trees, lighting, public spaces and stormwater features. the city describes the corridors as central roadways in a historically disinvested neighborhood and says the project was being developed with local residents and community organizations (https://www.boston.gov/departments/transportation/project/roxbury-resilient-corridors). according to a sept. 28 announcement from u.s. rep. ayanna pressley and mayor michelle wu, the city argues that dot cited the project's electric-vehicle charging infrastructure as the reason for canceling the entire award. the announcement says that charging work represented less than 1% of project costs and that federal transportation officials had previously encouraged the city to include it. those are the city's allegations about the cancellation; the lawsuit has not produced a merits ruling (https://pressley.house.gov/2026/09/28/pressley-wu-announce-lawsuit-against-trump-administration-to-protect-20-million-federal-grant-for-roxburys-street-improvements/). the guardian independently reported that the case names transportation secretary sean duffy and was filed against the department over its decision to withdraw the money. the newspaper also reported the city's position that federal agencies cannot rewrite a program established by congress to match changing political priorities. the department's response was not included in the sources reviewed for this report, so the federal government's legal defense and any timetable for responding remain unclear (https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/28/boston-sues-trump-administration). the stakes are practical for people who use roxbury's busiest routes. boston says the project is meant to improve bus service, walking, bicycling and driving conditions at the same time. on its warren street project page, the city says more than 20,250 mbta riders use that corridor each weekday and that delays can add as much as 20 minutes to an average rider's day. that page predates the lawsuit and describes the broader transit problem rather than measuring the effect of the canceled grant, but it helps explain why the city treats the award as more than a budget line (https://content.boston.gov/departments/transportation/project/warren-street-bus-priority-corridor). roxbury's transportation history also shapes the argument. boston says the wide, vehicle-oriented corridors were created through eminent-domain and block-clearance decisions during the urban-renewal era of the 1960s. the city's current plan calls for safer crossings, more comfortable bus stops, shade and cooling spaces, bike infrastructure and green stormwater measures, while also trying to prevent displacement and support small businesses. those goals remain proposed project features, not completed improvements. the case arrives after the city had already approved accepting the federal award. a boston city council record from april 2025 describes the $20 million raise grant as funding to reconstruct and upgrade the three roxbury corridors. the city now says the cancellation removed a major share of the money needed to move the work forward. whether the court restores the award, sends the decision back to dot for reconsideration or leaves the cancellation in place will determine what boston can realistically build and when (https://content.boston.gov/departments/city-council/city-council-roll-call-votes?page=39). for now, the lawsuit is an opening filing, not a ruling that boston has won or that dot acted unlawfully. the public docket shows the case was assigned to u.s. district judge richard g. stearns after an initial judge recused himself on the filing date. the next meaningful developments will be the federal defendants' response, any request for interim relief and the court's decision on how the administrative procedure act claims proceed. until then, the three-corridor project remains listed by boston as pending, with its completion year undetermined. massachusetts massachusetts massachusetts ma boston roxbury, boston massachusetts boston roxbury transportation federal grants public transit infrastructure"
    },
    "ct-kosta-diamantis-sentenced-2026-09-30": {
      "excerpt": "Kosta Diamantis was sentenced in absentia for extortion, bribery and false-statement convictions tied to school-construction contracts; federal authorities are still seeking him.",
      "id": "ct-kosta-diamantis-sentenced-2026-09-30",
      "text": "former connecticut official sentenced to 7 years after fleeing to greece kosta diamantis was sentenced in absentia for extortion, bribery and false-statement convictions tied to school-construction contracts; federal authorities are still seeking him. former connecticut school-construction official konstantinos “kosta” diamantis was sentenced wednesday to 84 months in federal prison and three years of supervised release, but he was not in the bridgeport courtroom to hear the punishment. the 70-year-old former state official had fled to greece before sentencing, and the u.s. attorney’s office for the district of connecticut said law enforcement is seeking him. the federal announcement is at https://www.justice.gov/usao-ct/pr/former-state-official-sentenced-7-years-federal-prison-school-construction-extortion-and. u.s. district judge stefan r. underhill imposed the sentence for convictions involving extortion, bribery, conspiracy and false statements. a federal jury in bridgeport found diamantis guilty in october 2025 on 21 counts: two counts of extortion, two extortion-conspiracy counts, two bribery counts, two bribery-conspiracy counts and 13 counts of making false statements to federal agents, according to the justice department. connecticut public reported that the case grew out of diamantis’s work as the state’s former deputy budget director and director of the office of school construction grants and review. that office administered grants for connecticut public-school construction projects seeking state funding. prosecutors said diamantis used the position to demand and receive payments from contractors while helping them obtain or preserve work. the justice department identified projects involving a school project in tolland, state reimbursement work for new britain and renovations at hartford’s bulkeley high school. the release says one contractor paid diamantis with cash and checks and hired his daughter at an inflated salary; those details describe the conduct underlying the convictions, not new untested allegations. the sentence came after an unusual break in the normal criminal-justice process. diamantis had been scheduled to appear for sentencing in september but did not attend. ct mirror reported that his lawyer told the court diamantis had left the united states and was in greece, where he holds dual citizenship. the later sentencing could proceed without him, but his absence leaves the practical question of how and when he will be taken into custody. federal officials have not announced that he has returned to the united states. the financial consequences are not settled. the justice department said judge underhill will determine restitution after additional court proceedings. that decision could identify losses that the government says were caused by the scheme, but the sentencing announcement did not give a final restitution amount. the unresolved figure matters because the projects at issue involved public school construction and state reimbursement, placing the case's consequences beyond the contractors and former official who appeared in court. the prison term was also shorter than the range sought by prosecutors. ct mirror reported that the government recommended a guideline range of 121 to 151 months, while the judge imposed 84 months. the difference does not change the convictions or the three-year supervised-release term, but it will be part of the record as prosecutors pursue diamantis and the court addresses restitution. ct mirror’s sentencing report is at https://ctmirror.org/2026/09/30/kosta-diamantis-still-a-fugitive-sentenced-to-84-months/. the case is not finished for the other people involved in the investigation. the justice department said contractors identified as duffy, monarca and roy pleaded guilty to related charges and are awaiting sentencing. the investigation was conducted by the fbi and irs criminal investigation, and federal prosecutors said the bribery scheme involved multimillion-dollar school-construction projects. connecticut public’s account of the sentencing, including the fact that diamantis remained outside the country, is at https://www.ctpublic.or…16613 tokens truncated…ll address larger infrastructure projects valued above $75,000 and up to $750,000 through a competitive grant process. receiving a phase-one payment will not by itself bar an applicant from phase two, but the second phase cannot pay for losses already covered by phase one or another funding source. the outstanding questions for producers are how many businesses will apply, how much documented loss the program will face and how quickly the state can turn the october filing window into payments. new-england new-england new england vt montpelier vermont vermont agriculture timber disaster assistance flood recovery weather losses farm economy"
