    "vt-uvm-drink-spiking-investigation-2026-10-01": {
      "excerpt": "A Sept. 30 alert says the reports involve off-campus locations, a cash reward is available for information, and temporary weekend screening support will be offered to students in October.",
      "id": "vt-uvm-drink-spiking-investigation-2026-10-01",
      "text": "uvm police investigate alleged drink-spiking reports in burlington a sept. 30 alert says the reports involve off-campus locations, a cash reward is available for information, and temporary weekend screening support will be offered to students in october. university of vermont police services is investigating reports that people may have been given incapacitating drugs without their knowledge at off-campus locations in burlington, prompting a campus safety alert and a request for information from students and the broader community. the sept. 30 alert says uvm police and safety teams received reports of alleged incapacitating or so-called date-rape drug incidents in the burlington area. it does not identify the locations, disclose what substances may have been involved or say how many people reported incidents. the alert says criminal investigations are ongoing and that uvm is offering a substantial cash reward for information that leads to the apprehension of a suspect or suspects. anyone with information is asked to call uvm police at 802-656-3473. ([uvm police alert](https://content.getrave.com/artifacts/api/v1/riki/alert/2645895/moreinfofile/3e2e5059-d183-4ba2-a0d6-d4fc2bc6acd8)) local reporting adds limited context but does not resolve those unknowns. [wcax reported](https://www.wcax.com/2026/09/30/uvm-police-warn-drink-spiking-offer-reward-information/) that sources described three or four incidents at different locations. [wptz reported](https://www.mynbc5.com/article/uvm-police-investigate-alleged-date-rape-drug-incidents/73967944), citing uvm chief safety and compliance officer michael schirling, that officials believed the reports involved multiple distinct events, multiple people and more than one location. those descriptions are part of an active investigation, not a court finding that a crime occurred in any particular case. burlington police told wcax they had not seen a trend of these incidents. that distinction matters: uvm’s alert is a warning about allegations under investigation, not a declaration that a citywide pattern has been established. the reports also concern off-campus places, so the alert reaches beyond the university’s property and asks anyone who may have information to come forward. uvm urged people attending parties or visiting bars and restaurants to avoid leaving drinks unattended, reject open drinks from others and seek help quickly if they feel suddenly weak, confused or otherwise unwell. uvm’s existing [incapacitating-drug screening initiative](https://www.uvm.edu/dsc/incapacitating-drug-screening-initiative), operated with uvm medical center, is designed for students who believe they were drugged but do not necessarily suspect a sexual assault. the university says testing is most useful when obtained promptly because some substances leave the body quickly. the program says student health services can provide screening, and that testing may be tracked anonymously on request. if sexual assault is suspected, uvm directs people to the uvm medical center emergency department for examination and treatment. the new alert adds a short-term change for october: weekend on-call support will be available for screening, giving students an option besides going directly to an emergency department. uvm says students can contact police services and be connected with a student health services medical provider through the center for health and well being. the university also says reporting does not force a person into a full investigation; its screening guidance describes options ranging from a full report to sharing only basic timing and location information that may help investigators identify patterns. the next steps are investigative. uvm police are seeking tips, but the alert does not announce an arrest, identify a suspect or establish whether the allegations are connected. students and community members who feel unsafe or witness a crime in progress should call 911; people seeking confidential support can contact hope works, which uvm lists as a 24-hour resource at 802-863-1236. until investigators release more information, the confirmed public facts are the alert, the request for tips and the temporary screening support—not a confirmed count of victims or a proven explanation for what happened. new-england new-england new england vt burlington burlington, vermont vermont burlington university of vermont public safety campus safety crime investigation"
    },
    "vt-williston-ice-protest-federal-charges-2026-10-01": {
      "excerpt": "Five people are accused of repeatedly blocking access to the Vermont facility, while separate state trespass cases are moving forward against protesters arrested this summer.",
      "id": "vt-williston-ice-protest-federal-charges-2026-10-01",
      "text": "federal charges widen legal fight over williston ice facility protests five people are accused of repeatedly blocking access to the vermont facility, while separate state trespass cases are moving forward against protesters arrested this summer. federal prosecutors have charged five people accused of repeatedly blocking access to an immigration and customs enforcement facility in williston, opening a new legal track in a protest campaign that has already produced separate state trespass cases. the u.s. attorney’s office for the district of vermont said sept. 30 that peter booth, 58, of jericho; lexington kennedy, 22, of new york city; henry prensky, 80, of burlington; roan wade, 23, of red hook, new york; and sherri wormser, 57, of colchester were charged by information with obstructing access to the ice national criminal analysis and targeting center. the facility is in the white cap business park in williston, according to the [department of justice announcement](https://www.justice.gov/usao-vt/pr/five-people-charged-obstructing-access-federal-facility-williston-vermont). the allegations involve three protest dates. prosecutors say all five entered the building’s atrium on feb. 9 and used their bodies to block doors leading to the federal facility after warnings to disperse. the justice department says booth and prensky did so again on may 14, while kennedy and wade repeated the conduct on july 24. in each instance, the release says, access was restored after law enforcement removed protesters from the building. the federal case is narrower than a general challenge to protest activity: the charging document alleges obstruction of entrances and exits, not simply opposition to immigration policy. first assistant u.s. attorney jonathan ophardt said the blockades created safety risks for people working at the facility and required a substantial police response. those are the government’s allegations, not findings by a court. the defendants have not yet been scheduled for initial appearances. the justice department said each faces up to 30 days in prison and a fine of up to $5,000 if convicted, with any sentence to be set by the court under the applicable factors. the agency also said the five had not retained or been appointed counsel when the announcement was issued. all five are presumed innocent unless and until proven guilty. the charges add to a separate vermont prosecution arising from a july 24 demonstration at the same office complex. vermont attorney general charity clark filed unlawful-trespass cases against 13 people arrested during that sit-in; twelve pleaded not guilty at a sept. 24 arraignment, and the case of an 18-year-old was transferred to family court. [vermont public’s reporting](https://www.vermontpublic.org/local-news/2026-09-24/vermont-attorney-general-charges-protesters-for-trespassing-at-ice-surveillance-facility) said the state charge carries a maximum of three months in prison, a $500 fine, or both. vermont public reported oct. 1 that three of the five people named in the federal case also face state charges. the outlet described the federal action as an escalation after chittenden county state’s attorney sarah george previously declined to prosecute some protesters arrested during sit-ins, while clark’s office later took up the july cases. that split has made charging decisions part of the local debate over how vermont should respond to civil disobedience at the facility. for williston, the immediate issue is practical as well as legal. the white cap complex has become a recurring site of demonstrations, arrests and competing claims about public safety, federal immigration enforcement and protest rights. the next concrete step in the federal matter is the defendants’ initial court appearance; until then, the charging information establishes what prosecutors allege but does not resolve whether the conduct violated federal law or how the cases will affect future demonstrations. new-england new-england new england vt williston williston, vermont vermont williston immigration enforcement protests federal courts public safety"
